 ],
 "literal": "Like a vayalai vine withered waist / distressed mount pale Brahmin / at night having come, without stopping, having entered / spoken words indeed were few, for that itself / ladders and defensive barriers having removed / excellent-deeded elephants even bells were removed.",
 "translation": "With a waist as thin as a withered vayalai vine, riding a slow, weary mount, the pale Brahmin arrived in the dark of night, entered without stopping, and spoke only a very few words. Yet, because of those few words, the siege ladders and defensive barriers were turned back, and the bells were stripped from the war-elephants of glorious deeds.",
 "modern": "வாடிய வயலைக் கொடியைப் போன்ற மெலிந்த இடையையும், மெதுவாகச் செல்லும் வாகனத்தையும், வெளுத்த உடலையும் உடைய பார்ப்பான், இரவில் வந்து, எங்கும் நில்லாமல் நேராக உள்ளே நுழைந்து, சில சொற்களைத்தான் பேசினான். அவன் பேசிய அந்தச் சில சொற்களுக்காகவே, கோட்டையை முற்றுகையிட வைத்திருந்த ஏணிகளும் கதவுக் கணைய மரங்களும் அகற்றப்பட்டன; சிறந்த போர்த் தொழிலைச் செய்யும் யானைகளின் கழுத்து மணிகளும் அவிழ்த்து விடப்பட்டன.",
 "explanation": "This poem belongs to the Vākai tiṇai and specifically the Pārppaṉa vākai tuṟai, which celebrates the victory or excellence of a Brahmin mediator. The poet Madurai Vēḷācāṉ describes a frail, pale Brahmin messenger who travels on a slow, weary mount to negotiate peace between warring kings. He arrives in the middle of the night and enters the camp directly without hesitation. Though he speaks only a few words, his diplomatic skill is so powerful that the entire siege is called off. The ladders meant for scaling the fort walls and the defensive barriers are dismantled, and the bells are removed from the war-elephants, signaling the end of hostilities. The contrast between the messenger's weak physical appearance and the massive political impact of his brief words highlights the power of peaceful diplomacy over military might.",
